       * Run 3: 1443.874 (1235.463, 1879.135)

As you can see, the model predictions using the classic algorithm in INLA 23.09.09 are significantly different from those of the compact algorithm in INLA 23.09.09. However, in the new version of INLA, the classic algorithm's predictions have increased to be the same as the compact algorithm's predictions. From these prediction values, the classic algorithm in INLA 24.02.09 seems to be different from that in INLA 24.02.09. Looks like it has been modified according to the compact algorithm.

Q1. The model predictions involve the intercept, which is confounding with the random field. Because the intercept is not identifiable, the predictions will be sensitive to the confounding and unreliable. Do I read your response right?

Q2. Following up on Q1, how does the confounding influence the predictions? From the predictions above, only are the predictions of Classic23 different from the others (i.e. Compact23, Compact24 and Classic24). Does this really reflect the influence of the confounding? Do I expect to see extensive prediction CIs and overlap each other? However, I don't see overlaps. Does the confounding between the intercept and the spatial random play a critical role in shifting the model prediction significantly?

Q3. You said "The intercept is very rarely identifiable on its own, so one should expect the details of the estimates to vary." Is this because of intrinsic conditions and limitations in INLA? Because the intercept is very rarely identifiable, in the INLA framework (?), it is better to make model predictions without the intercept. Do I misread your response?

Q4. Regarding the performance between the classic and compact algorithms and between the two INLA versions, you suggest the two algorithms (i.e. the classic and compact algorithms) in INLA 24.02.09 are more robust and reliable, and the compact algorithm is better. Do I comprehend your message right?

I appreciate your patience with my monotonous inquiries.

Best,
Wen-Hsi

