But simply for my understanding, you are saying a model with multiple groups should be: coordinates ~ Intercept + Common spatial field + B_k* Spatial field_k,
where B_k is a scaling parameter.
And then in the different likelihood formulas, I would iteratively add the B_k*Spatial_field_k for each group?

Thank you very much for the help!
Edgar

Le lundi 22 avril 2024 à 12:37:49 UTC-4, Finn Lindgren a écrit :
> The formula expression should match the mathematical model definitions. I don’t think this one matches any of the alternatives in the thread. For example, one would normally only involve a single intercept in each expression. Also, you have to decide if the scaling of each field should be the same in all the models, or if there should be scaling parameters. For that, use the “copy” feature, or make a nonlinear predictor expression with the scaling parameters as latent components. I’m not sure if there’s a full example of that out there…
> Finn
>
> > On 22 Apr 2024, at 17:29, Edgar Lanoue <edgar...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > ﻿Thank you,
> >
> > And if I add fixed effects for each group, I would have components : coordinates ~ Intercept_1(1) + ... + Intercept_k(1) + CommonSpatialField(...) +  SpatialField_1(...) + ... + SpatialField_k(...).
> >
> > I believe I would have to specify a likelihood for each group.
> > The likelyhood formula for the first group would be:  intercept_1 + CommonSPatialField + Spatial field_1.
> > And then I add the next groups' likelihood formulas iteratively like this:  (intercept_1 + CommonSpatialField + SpatialField_1) +  (intercept_2 + SpatialField_2) + ... +  (intercept_k + SpatialField_k).
> > Is this right?
> >
> > Edgar Lanoue
> >
> >
> >
> > Le lundi 22 avril 2024 à 11:52:22 UTC-4, Finn Lindgren a écrit :
> > > Yes, they look like group-specific intercepts.
> > > So either use one fixed effect for each, or a single joint iid random effect (if you fix the precision of that it works even for a small number of groups.
> > > +Int1(1)+Int2(1)
> > > Or
> > > +Int(groupindex)
> > > Finn
> > >
> > > > On 22 Apr 2024, at 16:35, Edgar Lanoue <edgar...@gmail.com> wrote:
